Yes! In fact, you should do this when your Oracle table has 100+ columns. You don't need to display all 100 columns to the user.

The best approach is to let the backend retrieve the metadata, then let the frontend display only the columns you want to make available in the report builder.

There are two ways to do this.

## Option 1: Fetch all metadata, display only selected columns

Oracle returns metadata for all 100+ columns, but the frontend displays only the columns you allow.

For example, your table might contain:

| Oracle column     | Display in report builder? |
| ----------------- | -------------------------- |
| PROJECT_ID        | Yes                        |
| PROJECT_NAME      | Yes                        |
| START_DATE        | Yes                        |
| COST              | Yes                        |
| INTERNAL_NOTES    | No                         |
| CREATED_BY        | No                         |
| UPDATED_TIMESTAMP | No                         |
| ... 100+ columns  | ...                        |

You can maintain a list of permitted column names in C#:

```
List<string> allowedColumns = new List<string>();

allowedColumns.Add("PROJECT_ID");
allowedColumns.Add("PROJECT_NAME");
allowedColumns.Add("START_DATE");
allowedColumns.Add("COST");
allowedColumns.Add("CATEGORY");
allowedColumns.Add("PLACE");
allowedColumns.Add("STATUS");
```

Then, when reading Oracle metadata, only add columns present in this list:

```
while (reader.Read())
{
    string columnName = reader["COLUMN_NAME"].ToString();

    if (allowedColumns.Contains(columnName))
    {
        // Create ColumnInfo and add it to the list.
    }
}
```

This is a simple approach, but you still retrieve metadata for all columns from Oracle.

## Option 2: Ask Oracle for only the columns you allow

I recommend this approach for your current report builder.

You can filter the metadata query itself so Oracle returns only the columns you want.

```
string sql =
    "SELECT COLUMN_NAME, DATA_TYPE " +
    "FROM USER_TAB_COLUMNS " +
    "WHERE TABLE_NAME = 'PROJECT_DETAILS' " +
    "AND COLUMN_NAME IN (" +
    "'PROJECT_ID', " +
    "'PROJECT_NAME', " +
    "'START_DATE', " +
    "'COST', " +
    "'CATEGORY', " +
    "'PLACE', " +
    "'STATUS') " +
    "ORDER BY COLUMN_ID";
```

Now, even if the Oracle table has 150 columns, this query returns metadata for just those seven columns.

Your existing code can continue processing the results using `OracleDataReader`, creating `ColumnInfo` objects, and returning them to the frontend.

## How the complete flow works

Oracle: `PROJECT_DETAILS`

150 columns

C# metadata query

Returns only 7 approved columns

AJAX response

Only the approved column metadata is sent to JavaScript

Attributes panel

The user sees and selects from those 7 columns

One important security detail: you must also validate selected columns inside `GenerateReport()`. Hiding a column from the frontend alone does not prevent someone from manually sending its name to your backend.

For your current single-table setup, I would maintain an approved-column list in C#, use it for both `LoadReportMetadata()` and `GenerateReport()`, and keep the frontend completely dynamic. Later, when you add more tables, you can maintain a separate approved-column list for each table.
